For instance, if two threads try to create a binding object simultaneously, one thread may overwrite the binding created by the other thread. This can result in inconsistent state and cause issues when subsequent requests or invocations are made.

To resolve this, it's necessary to introduce synchronization or thread-safe mechanisms in the getBinding method. This ensures that only one thread can create and initialize the binding object at a given time.

Here's an example of a thread-safe implementation for the getBinding method: public CxfRsBinding getBinding() {
    synchronized (this) {
        if (binding == null) {
            binding = new CxfRsBinding();
            LOG.debug("Create default CXF Binding " + binding);
        }
    }
    if (binding instanceof SomeClass) {
        // Additional logic if needed
    }
    return binding;
} By using synchronization with a lock on the instance (this), we guarantee that only one thread can access the critical section where the binding object is created. This prevents race conditions and ensures consistent behavior.

